 MDC (Multi-Dimensional Cell) Octree Isosurface Extraction is an adaptive surface reconstruction algorithm that uses an octree data structure to recursively subdivide a 3D volume and generate a triangle mesh. It solves the problem of extracting isosurfaces from volumetric data with varying density, handling topology via vertex clustering and collapse logic to ensure manifold connectivity. It sets itself apart from its sibling [DMC Octree Contour Extraction](3661-dmc-octree-contour-extraction.md) by explicitly managing vertex clustering across shared edges and faces to resolve topological ambiguities in complex subdivisions, whereas DMC relies on a different contour extraction logic.
 
+### [Multi-Resolution Dual Marching Cubes with Manifold Preservation](6571-multi-resolution-dual-marching-cubes-with-manifold-preservat.md)
+Multi-Resolution Dual Marching Cubes with Manifold Preservation (MR-DMC) is a surface extraction method that generates closed, manifold meshes from segmented volumetric data using a generalized octree. It extends Dual Marching Cubes (DMC) by introducing a cell-based criterion for dual vertex generation to prevent non-manifold configurations, specifically addressing ambiguities where standard DMC cases 17 and 20 share an ambiguous face. Unlike Manifold Dual Contouring (MDC), which uses a bottom-up strategy, MR-DMC employs a top-down refinement strategy suitable for large datasets, and unlike standard DMC, it guarantees manifold surfaces without post-processing.
+
